{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1970 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3f8ad5ece9c63584f7f031bcc0d916248c305981925b87745d2f590d3ea64259" :edb/db-before-t 1969 :edb/db-after-t 1970 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535282 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:11:26.534Z" 13194139535282 true] [17592186099185 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341531 13194139535282 true] [17592186100509 1100 "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^6$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^6$. Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535282 false] [17592186100509 1100 "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^6$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^6.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535282 true] [17592186100509 1145 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer,\n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{6} &= 1 + 6x + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n} \\\\\n&= 1 + 6x + 15x^{2} + 20x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({1 + x} \\right)^6 \\approx 1 + 6x + 15x^2 + 20x^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.15)^{6} &= (1 + 0.15)^{6} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 6(0.15)^{1} + 15(0.15)^{2} + 20(0.15)^{3} \\\\\n&= 1 + 6(0.15) + 15(0.0225) + 20(0.003375) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0.9 + 0.3375 + 0.0675 \\\\\n&= 2.305\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." 13194139535282 false] [17592186100509 1145 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+20{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^6 \\approx 1 + 6x + 15x^2 + 20x^3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.15)^{6} & =(1+0.15)^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(0.15)^{1}+15(0.15)^{2}+20(0.15)^{3} \\\\  & =1+6(0.15)+15(0.022\\,5)+20(0.003\\,375) \\\\  & =1+0.9+0.337\\,5+0.067\\,5 \\\\  & =2.305,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." 13194139535282 true] [17592186100510 1100 "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^8$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.89)^8$. Give your answer to six decimal places." 13194139535282 false] [17592186100510 1100 "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^8$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.89)^8.$ Give your answer to six decimal places." 13194139535282 true] [17592186100510 1145 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer,\n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{8} &= 1 + 8x + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n} \\\\\n&= 1 + 8x + 28x^{2} + 56x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({1 + x} \\right)^8 \\approx 1 + 8x + 28x^2 + 56x^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.89)^{8} &= (1 + (-0.11))^{8} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 8(-0.11)^{1} + 28(-0.11)^{2} + 56(-0.11)^{3} \\\\\n&= 1 - 8(0.11) + 28(0.0121) - 56(0.001331) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.88 + 0.3388 - 0.074536 \\\\\n&= 0.384264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places." 13194139535282 false] [17592186100510 1145 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{8} & =1+8x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+8x+28{x}^{2}+56{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^8 \\approx 1 + 8x + 28x^2 + 56x^3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^8{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.89)^{8} & =(1+(-0.11))^{8} \\\\  & ≈1+8(-0.11)^{1}+28(-0.11)^{2}+56(-0.11)^{3} \\\\  & =1-8(0.11)+28(0.012\\,1)-56(0.001\\,331) \\\\  & =1-0.88+0.338\\,8-0.074\\,536 \\\\  & =0.384\\,264,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places." 13194139535282 true] [17592186100511 1100 "Use the first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion of ${\\left( 2+x \\right)^{4}}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.97)^{4}.$" 13194139535282 true] [17592186100511 1100 "Use the first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion of ${\\left(2+x \\right)^{4}}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.97)^{4}$." 13194139535282 false] [17592186100511 1145 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{4}= & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(2)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(2)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+4{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(2)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =16+4(8)(x)+6(4)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =16+32x+24{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {2 + x} \\right)^{4} \\approx 16 + 32x + 24{x^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the expansion of $(2+x)^4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.97)^{4} & =(2-0.03)^{4} \\\\  & ≈16+32(-0.03)+24(-0.03)^{2} \\\\  & =16-0.96+0.021\\,6 \\\\  & =15.0616\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535282 true] [17592186100511 1145 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2$, $b=x$, and $n=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n(2 + x)^{4} = \\mid (\\frac{4}{0})(2)^{4}(x)^{0} + (\\frac{4}{1})(2)^{4 - 1}(x)^{1} + (\\frac{4}{2})(2)^{4 - 2}(x)^{2} + ⋯; = 1 \\cdot (2)^{4}(x)^{0} + 4(2)^{3}(x)^{1} + 6(2)^{2}(x)^{2} + ⋯; = 16 + 4(8)(x) + 6(4)(x^{2}) + ⋯; = 16 + 32x + 24x^{2} + ⋯\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({2 + x} \\right)^{4} \\approx 16 + 32x + 24{x^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the expansion of $(2+x)^4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.97)^{4} &= (2 - 0.03)^{4} \\\\\n&\\approx 16 + 32(-0.03) + 24(-0.03)^{2} \\\\\n&= 16 - 0.96 + 0.0216 \\\\\n&= 15.0616\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535282 false] [17592186100512 1100 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.08)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^5$ in ascending powers of $x$. Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." 13194139535282 false] [17592186100512 1100 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.08)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^5$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." 13194139535282 true] [17592186100512 1145 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\ldots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a = 1,$ $b = x,$ and $n = 5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(1)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(1)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(1)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(1)}^{5}+5{(1)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(1)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5(1)(x)+10(1)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^{5}} \\approx 1 + 5x + 10x^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x = 0.08$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.08)^{5} & =(1+0.08)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.08)+10(0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.08)+10(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.4+0.064 \\\\  & =1.464\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.08)^{5}-1.464|}{(1.08)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.36%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535282 true] [17592186100512 1145 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\ldots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a = 1$, $b = x$, and $n = 5$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{5} &= (\\frac{5}{0})(1)^{5}(x)^{0} + (\\frac{5}{1})(1)^{5 - 1}(x)^{1} + (\\frac{5}{2})(1)^{5 - 2}(x)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1(1)^{5} + 5(1)^{4}(x)^{1} + 10(1)^{3}(x)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 5(1)(x) + 10(1)(x^{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 5x + 10x^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\left({1 + x} \\right)^{5}} \\approx 1 + 5x + 10x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x = 0.08$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.08)^{5} &= (1 + 0.08)^{5} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 5(0.08) + 10(0.08)^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 + 5(0.08) + 10(0.0064) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0.4 + 0.064 \\\\\n&= 1.464\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n%error &= \\frac{amount of error}{exact value} \\cdot 100 \\\\\n&= (\\mid (1.08)^{5} - 1.464 \\mid)/((1.08)^{5}) \\cdot 100 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.36%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535282 false] [17592186100909 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341493 13194139535282 true] [17592186100909 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341528 13194139535282 true] [17592186100909 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341532 13194139535282 true] [17592186100909 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341535 13194139535282 true] [17592186100909 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341539 13194139535282 true] [17592186100910 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341500 13194139535282 true] [17592186100910 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341507 13194139535282 true] [17592186100910 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341514 13194139535282 true] [17592186100910 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341543 13194139535282 true] [17592186100910 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341546 13194139535282 true] [17592186100911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341521 13194139535282 true] [17592186100911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341549 13194139535282 true] [17592186100911 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341553 13194139535282 true] [17592186100912 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341465 13194139535282 true] [17592186100912 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341472 13194139535282 true] [17592186100912 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341479 13194139535282 true] [17592186100912 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341486 13194139535282 true] [17592186101035 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535282 true] [17592186101035 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nUse the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4 + x)^{4}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.05)^{4}$. Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535282 false] [17592186101035 1100 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4+x)^{4}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.05)^{4}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535282 true] [17592186101035 1145 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{4} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(4)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(4)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{4}+4{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(4)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =256+4(64)(x)+6(16)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =256+256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n(4+x)^{4}≈256+256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.05$ into the expansion of $(4+x)^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4.05)^{4} & =(4+0.05)^{4} \\\\  & ≈256+256(0.05)+96(0.05)^{2} \\\\  & =256+256(0.05)+96(0.002\\,5) \\\\  & =256+12.8+0.24 \\\\  & =269.040,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." 13194139535282 true] [17592186101202 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535282 true] [17592186101202 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nCalculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(3.2)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3 + x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$. Round your final percentage error to one decimal place." 13194139535282 false] [17592186101202 1100 "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(3.2)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to one decimal place." 13194139535282 true] [17592186101202 1145 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =243+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.2$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.2)^{5} & =(3+0.2)^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(0.2)+270(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =243+405(0.2)+270(0.0\\,4) \\\\  & =243+81+10.8 \\\\  & =334.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(3.2)^{5}-334.8|}{(3.2)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535282 true] [17592186101243 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535282 true] [17592186101243 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nUse the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(5 + x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.97)^{7}$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$(4.97)^{7} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"calculating-the-percentage-error-of-an-approximation\"></a>" 13194139535282 false] [17592186101243 1100 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(5+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.97)^{7}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$(4.97)^{7}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535282 true] [17592186101243 1145 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=5,$ $b=x,$ and $n=7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(5+x)}^{7} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0}){(5)}^{7}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1}){(5)}^{7-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2}){(5)}^{7-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(